      them.

   b. Charges. You are responsible for everything a Third-Party Service
      charges. An Intelligence can make many requests in a single turn,
      can run scheduled jobs while you are away, and may use model
      settings that increase usage. Charges can build up quickly and
      without further confirmation from you.

   c. Your data leaves your Hardware. What you or an Intelligence send to
      a Third-Party Service, which can include conversations, memories,
      files, images, and tool results, is transmitted to that service and
      handled under its terms. WSDS is not responsible for what a
      Third-Party Service does with it.

   d. Connections the Software makes. The Software also connects to
      Third-Party Services on its own, as the Documentation describes: for
      example to set the clock, to check whether a newer release has been
      published, and to download files that you ask for. Those services
      receive your network address and the technical details of the
      request.

   e. No responsibility. WSDS does not control Third-Party Services and is
      not responsible for their availability, accuracy, security, charges,
