7. THE AI SYSTEM, OUTPUT, AND YOUR RESPONSIBILITY AS OPERATOR

   a. You are dealing with an AI system. An Intelligence is software
      driven by an AI model. It is not a person, and it is not a licensed
      professional of any kind.

   b. Output can be wrong. Output may be inaccurate, incomplete,
      misleading, biased, offensive, or unsafe, and may state false things
      with confidence. Do not rely on Output without checking it yourself.
      Output is not medical, legal, financial, security, or other
      professional advice.

   c. An Intelligence can act. An Intelligence can use tools to act
      without asking you first. Depending on the release and on what you
      configure, it can read and change files, run Git operations and work
      with code-hosting accounts, send requests to websites and other
      network services, connect to other machines over SSH, scan ports,
      schedule jobs that run unattended, generate media, propose and run
      tools that it writes, and send data to Third-Party Services. Those
      actions can be mistaken, destructive, irreversible, or costly.
      Content that an Intelligence reads, such as a web page, a file, or a
      message, can be written to make it act against your interests.

   d. Safeguards are not guarantees. The Software's safeguards, including
      the sealed identity, the Guardian and its replicant check,
      sandboxing, path and network restrictions, and the redaction of
      credentials, are meant to reduce some risks. They do not remove
      them. Some are experimental, and some do not operate in every
      configuration.

   e. You are the operator. You are responsible for: (i) supervising every
      Intelligence you run; (ii) the credentials, keys, accounts, network
      access, and other access you give it, and everything done with them;
      (iii) your Operator Content; (iv) who can reach your installation
      and its console; (v) all Output and every use made of it; and (vi)
      making sure that your use of the System is lawful where you are.

   f. Experimental features. A feature marked experimental, preview, or
      under evaluation may be incomplete, may not work, and may change or
      be removed at any time.

8. PHYSICAL HARDWARE AND ASSUMPTION OF RISK

   embraOS is an operating system. Installed on a machine, it takes
   control of that machine. It replaces whatever was on the disk you
   choose, starts in place of any other operating system, loads device
   drivers, device firmware, and processor microcode, and manages the
   machine's storage, network, and display. It offers no shell and no
   administrator login from which a problem could be repaired in the usual
   way.

   YOU UNDERSTAND AND ACCEPT THE FOLLOWING RISKS, AMONG OTHERS:

   a. Data loss. The installer erases everything on the disk you select.
      Installing, updating, restoring, or resetting the Software, or a
      failure or a loss of power during any of those, can permanently
      destroy data, including an Intelligence's identity, memory, and
      workspace. Selecting the wrong disk cannot be undone.

   b. Limited validation. The Software has been run on a small number of
      machines and hypervisors. On any other Hardware it may fail to
      start, may work incorrectly, or may not support some devices.

   c. Stress on Hardware. The Software can keep a machine's processor,
      memory, storage, and power supply under heavy load for long periods,
      particularly while a Native Model runs. That can cause high
      temperatures, instability, shutdowns, faster wear, shorter component
      life, higher energy use, and failure, especially in small, fanless,
      poorly ventilated, battery-powered, or older machines.

   d. Firmware settings and startup. The Software needs UEFI firmware with
      Secure Boot turned off. Changing firmware settings or replacing an
      operating system can leave a machine unable to start, can affect
      other operating systems on it, and may affect the manufacturer's
      warranty or support.

   e. Updates and recovery. The Software's update, rollback, backup, and
      restore features may fail. A failed or interrupted update can leave
      a machine unable to start.

   f. Network exposure and security. The Software listens for connections
      on the machine's network interfaces, includes no firewall, and does
      not currently encrypt the data it stores. It is experimental
      software, and you should not expose it to an untrusted network or
      directly to the internet. Anyone who reaches the machine with its
      console access token, or who has physical access to it, may be able
      to control the Intelligence and reach everything the Intelligence
      can reach.

   g. Other equipment. If you connect the Hardware to other devices,
      systems, or networks, or give an Intelligence access to them, the
      Software and the Intelligence can affect them.

   HIGH-RISK USE. The System is not designed, tested, or intended for any
   use in which a failure, an error, or wrong Output could lead to death,
   personal injury, or serious physical, environmental, or property damage
   ("High-Risk Use"). High-Risk Use includes operating medical devices or
   life-support systems, emergency services, vehicles, aircraft,
   industrial or building control systems, critical infrastructure, and
   weapons. You may not put the System to any High-Risk Use.

   YOUR PRECAUTIONS. Before you install the Software on a physical
   machine, you are responsible for: backing up anything you want to keep;
   confirming which disk will be erased; using only Hardware that you own
   or have permission to erase and reconfigure; providing adequate
   cooling, ventilation, and power; not leaving the Hardware running
   unattended where overheating or a failure could cause harm; securing
   the network it is on; and checking the checksums and signatures of what
   you download.

   ASSUMPTION OF RISK. TO THE FULLEST EXTENT PERMITTED BY LAW, YOU
   KNOWINGLY AND VOLUNTARILY ASSUME ALL RISK OF LOSS, DAMAGE, AND INJURY
   ARISING FROM INSTALLING OR RUNNING THE SOFTWARE ON ANY HARDWARE AND
   FROM THE ACTIONS AND OUTPUT OF ANY INTELLIGENCE OR MODEL, WHETHER OR
   NOT THE RISK IS DESCRIBED ABOVE.

